lucien-distributed/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/luciferase/lucien/forest/ghost/grpc/GhostCommunicationManager.java:492-499
Severity: Critical | Category: serialization

GhostLayerProviderImpl.removeGhostElement (lines 492-499) only logs ('Implementation would need to add removal capability to GhostLayer') and returns without removing anything. GhostExchangeServiceImpl.processStreamUpdate REMOVE case (GhostExchangeServiceImpl.java:330-336) sets success=true and the server then sends GhostAck success=true to the client (line 350). So a peer streaming a REMOVE believes the ghost was deleted, but the receiver keeps the stale element forever. This is an RDR-004-class silent data divergence: the wire reports success while the operation silently does nothing. Across a distributed run this leaves orphaned ghosts (entities that moved/were deleted on the owner rank) that are never reclaimed, corrupting neighbor/balance decisions that read the ghost layer.

Fix: Add a real removal path to GhostLayer (e.g. removeGhostElement(entityId, treeId)) and call it; if removal is genuinely unsupported, the REMOVE case must set success=false with an explicit errorMessage so the peer does not believe the delete landed. Do not ACK success for an operation that did nothing.

Verify evidence: Confirmed by reading all three cited sites.

GhostCommunicationManager.GhostLayerProviderImpl.removeGhostElement (lines 492-499) is a hollow no-op — it resolves the layer then only logs, never removing anything:
```
493  public void removeGhostElement(String entityId, long treeId) {
494      var layer = ghostLayers.get(treeId);
495      if (layer != null) {
496          // Implementation would need to add removal capability to GhostLayer
497          log.debug("Remove ghost element {} from tree {}", entityId, treeId);
498      }
499  }
```

GhostExchangeServiceImpl.processStreamUpdate REMOVE case (lines 330-336) unconditionally marks success after that no-op call:
```
330  case REMOVE -> {
331      var removal = update.getRemove();
332      ghostLayerProvider.removeGhostElement(removal.getEntityId(), removal.getSourceTreeId());
333      entityId = removal.getEntityId();
334      success = true;
335      log.debug("Removed ghost element: {}", entityId);
336  }
```
The server then builds and emits GhostAck with setSuccess(success) (success=true) at lines 344-350, so the streaming peer receives a success ACK while nothing was removed. The stale ghost element is kept forever, corruptin

