Fix Approach - Three Options:

Option A (Recommended): Capture view AFTER send
```java
try {
    sendToNeighbor(neighborId, message);  // Send first
    var sentViewId = membership.getCurrentViewId();  // Capture after successful send
} catch (TransportException e) {
    future.completeExceptionally(e);
    return future;
}
```
Pros: Minimal change, captures view at time of actual send
Cons: Small window where view could change during send (acceptable)

Option B: Atomic capture-and-send
```java
interface Transport {
    Pair<UUID, Result> sendWithViewCapture(UUID neighborId, Message msg);
}
```
Pros: True atomicity
Cons: API change, more invasive

Option C: Document as known limitation
Add to JavaDoc: 'Note: View changes during message transmission may cause false positive ACK failures. This is a known limitation of the current implementation.'
Pros: No code change
Cons: Doesn't fix false positives

Recommendation: Option A - swap lines 198 and 202.

Test: Inject view change between old line 198 and new line 202, verify no false positive.

Fix Approach - Two-part cleanup:

Part 1: Cleanup on retry exhaustion:
```java
if (attempt >= MAX_JOIN_RETRIES) {
    pendingJoinResponses.remove(joinerId);  // Explicit cleanup
    log.warn('JOIN retries exhausted for {}', joinerId);
    return;
}
```

Part 2: Periodic sweep for orphaned entries:
```java
// In constructor or initialization:
retryScheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
    long now = clock.currentTimeMillis();
    int removed = 0;
    pendingJoinResponses.entrySet().removeIf(e -> {
        if (now - e.getValue().firstAttemptTime > 60_000) {
            removed++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    });
    if (removed > 0) {
        log.debug('Swept {} orphaned JOIN responses', removed);
    }
}, 60, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
```

Alternative: Use Guava Cache with expireAfterWrite(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS) for automatic cleanup.

Test: 
1. Create JOIN, never respond, verify cleanup after 60s
2. Exhaust retries, verify immediate cleanup
3. Long-running test (10 min) with JOIN failures, verify no unbounded growth

Fix Approach - Atomic Holder Pattern:

1. Create immutable ClockContext holder:
```java
private static class ClockContext {
    final Clock clock;
    final MessageFactory factory;
    ClockContext(Clock clock) {
        this.clock = clock;
        this.factory = new MessageFactory(clock);
    }
}
```

2. Replace volatile fields with single volatile ClockContext:
```java
private volatile ClockContext clockContext = new ClockContext(Clock.system());
```

3. Update setClock to atomic swap:
```java
public void setClock(Clock clock) {
    this.clockContext = new ClockContext(clock);  // Single atomic write
}
```

4. Update all clock/factory access:
```java
var ctx = this.clockContext;  // Read once
long now = ctx.clock.currentTimeMillis();
var msg = ctx.factory.createJoinRequest(...);
```

Benefits: Atomic swap, no synchronization needed, guaranteed consistency.
Test: Concurrency test with 10 threads calling setClock while others send messages.

Fix Approach:
1. Wrap ScheduledExecutorService creation in try-finally
2. Move scheduler.shutdown() to finally block
3. Add null check before shutdown (scheduler may be null if creation failed)
4. Test with exception injection to verify cleanup

Code pattern:
```java
ScheduledExecutorService scheduler = null;
try {
    scheduler = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor(...);
    var pollingTask = scheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(...);
    // ... setup future
} finally {
    final var finalScheduler = scheduler;
    future.whenComplete((r, t) -> {
        if (finalScheduler != null && !finalScheduler.isShutdown()) {
            finalScheduler.shutdown();
        }
    });
}
```

Test: Inject RejectedExecutionException before scheduleAtFixedRate, verify scheduler.shutdown() still called.

