common/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/luciferase/common/IntArrayList.java:226-229
Severity: High | Category: Edge case / memory safety

Identical defect to FloatArrayList.removeRange: only `toIndex < fromIndex` is checked. toIndex > size yields a negative-length System.arraycopy and a negative size, corrupting the list. ShortArrayList carries the same copied implementation. This is a copy-paste family bug across all three primitive list specializations.

Fix: Add full bounds validation (fromIndex >= 0 && toIndex <= size) to IntArrayList.removeRange and ShortArrayList.removeRange to match ArrayList semantics.

Verify evidence: Confirmed by reading IntArrayList.java lines 226-233. `removeRange(int fromIndex, int toIndex)` validates ONLY `toIndex < fromIndex`:

```java
public void removeRange(int fromIndex, int toIndex) {
    if (toIndex < fromIndex) {
        throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException("toIndex < fromIndex");
    }
    System.arraycopy(array, toIndex, array, fromIndex, size - toIndex);
    size -= (toIndex - fromIndex);
}
```

No check for `fromIndex >= 0` or `toIndex <= size`. With `toIndex > size`, the arraycopy length `size - toIndex` is negative (arraycopy throws IndexOutOfBoundsException on negative length), and `size -= (toIndex - fromIndex)` corrupts the size field. With a negative `fromIndex`, arraycopy also throws / corrupts. This contrasts with the adjacent `remove(int index)` at line 217 which calls `ensureIndexInRange(index)`.

Family-bug claim also confirmed: FloatArrayList.java:181-187 and ShortArrayList.java:226-232 contain byte-for-byte identical implementations with the same single-sided check. ShortArrayList additionally has an inner override (line 74) delegating to super.

