## Context
Implement ClusteringStrategy using existing ClusteringDetector for data-driven split plane selection.

## Files to Create
- simulation/src/main/java/.../topology/ClusteringStrategy.java

## Implementation Details
```java
public class ClusteringStrategy implements SplitPlaneStrategy {
    private final ClusteringDetector detector;
    private final SplitPlaneStrategy fallback;

    public ClusteringStrategy(int minClusterSize, float maxDistance) {
        this.detector = new ClusteringDetector(minClusterSize, maxDistance);
        this.fallback = new RotatingAxisStrategy();
    }

    @Override
    public SplitPlane computeSplitPlane(UUID bubbleId, Point3f centroid, EnhancedBubble bubble) {
        var clusters = detector.detectClusters(bubble);
        if (clusters.size() < 2) {
            return fallback.computeSplitPlane(bubbleId, centroid);
        }

        // Compute split plane between two largest clusters
        var c1 = clusters.get(0).centroid();
        var c2 = clusters.get(1).centroid();
        return computePlaneBetweenClusters(c1, c2);
    }

    private SplitPlane computePlaneBetweenClusters(Point3f c1, Point3f c2) {
        // Normal = direction between centroids
        var dx = c2.x - c1.x;
        var dy = c2.y - c1.y;
        var dz = c2.z - c1.z;
        var len = (float) Math.sqrt(dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz);
        var normal = new Point3f(dx/len, dy/len, dz/len);

        // Distance = midpoint projected onto normal
        var midX = (c1.x + c2.x) / 2;
        var midY = (c1.y + c2.y) / 2;
        var midZ = (c1.z + c2.z) / 2;
        var distance = normal.x * midX + normal.y * midY + normal.z * midZ;

        return new SplitPlane(normal, distance, inferAxis(normal));
    }
}
```

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] ClusteringStrategy uses existing ClusteringDetector
- [ ] Falls back to RotatingAxisStrategy when no clear clusters
- [ ] Split plane perpendicular to cluster separation axis
- [ ] Unit tests for clustering-based split
- [ ] Performance benchmark vs rotation strategy

## Priority: P3 (Enhancement after core functionality works)
## Estimate: 3-4 hours

## P5: WebSocket Timeout Handling

### Problem Statement
WebSocket connections show 'Connection Idle Timeout' warnings in logs after ~30 seconds of inactivity. While this doesn't affect functionality during active testing, it creates log noise and could cause issues during longer-running demos.

### Root Cause
The Javalin WebSocket server uses default idle timeout settings. When no messages are exchanged for the timeout period, the connection is flagged as idle.

### Solution Options

#### Option A: Ping/Pong Heartbeat (Recommended)
Add periodic ping frames to keep connection alive:
```java
// In MultiBubbleVisualizationServer.java ws config
ws.onConnect(ctx -> {
    // Start heartbeat thread
    var heartbeat = new Thread(() -> {
        while (ctx.session.isOpen()) {
            try {
                ctx.session.getRemote().sendPing(ByteBuffer.allocate(0));
                Thread.sleep(15000); // 15 second interval
            } catch (Exception e) {
                break;
            }
        }
    });
    heartbeat.setDaemon(true);
    heartbeat.start();
});
```

#### Option B: Extend Idle Timeout
Configure longer timeout in WebSocket setup:
```java
ws.onConnect(ctx -> {
    ctx.session.setIdleTimeout(Duration.ofMinutes(5));
    // ... rest of setup
});
```

### Files to Modify
- `simulation/src/main/java/com/hellblazer/luciferase/simulation/viz/MultiBubbleVisualizationServer.java`
  - Lines ~89-120 (WebSocket configuration)

### Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] No 'Connection Idle Timeout' warnings in logs during 5-minute test runs
- [ ] WebSocket connections remain stable for extended demo sessions
- [ ] Minimal CPU overhead from heartbeat (if Option A chosen)
- [ ] Clean shutdown without heartbeat thread leaks

### Priority Rationale
P5 (lowest) - This is a polish item that reduces log noise but doesn't affect core functionality.

### Related
- Epic: Luciferase-cfr1
